{
  "case_id": "2008-08-88",
  "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nCHRISTINA RAINVILLE, ESQ., Chief Deputy State's Attorney, Bennington, Vt.; on behalf of the Petitioner.\nLEONDRA R. KRUGER, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting the Petitioner.\nWILLIAM A. NELSON, ESQ., Middlebury, Vt.; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Christina Rainville",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are many reasons to reverse the Vermont Supreme Court in this case. I am going to begin by addressing four reasons as a matter of law that do not require the Court to -- to review the record."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Isn't there -- isn't there that very important principle involved here that doesn't simply involve calculating particular periods of delay?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Christina Rainville",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The second is that the delays in this case were caused by non-State actors and as a matter of law under Polk County cannot be a constitutional violation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Christina Rainville",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The third is that, based on his concessions, his admissions in the brief, he's waived it; and that's under Barker v. Wingo where the Court talks about standard waiver doctrine applies in speedy trial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0001-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If you look on pages 27 through 28 of the\nVermont Supreme Court's opinion, it is clear that the\nVermont Supreme Court decided this case on the basis of\na single principle that it applied to the entirety of\nthe final two years of the pretrial delay in this case,\nand that is, as the Chief Justice has noted, that the\nfailure of defendant's series of assigned counsel to\nmove his case to trial should be attributable to the\nState."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. KRUGER",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First of all, we think that such a rule\nwould be inconsistent with the role of a lawyer in an\nadversarial system and would undermine a lawyer's\nauthority to make day-to-day decisions about the\nscheduling of pretrial proceedings."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. KRUGER",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. KRUGER: I think that Barker makes clear\nthat defendants can waive their right to a speedy trial\nand that indeed defendants will often desire to put off\ntrial as long as possible. I think that in giving\nproper consideration to the Barker Court's explanation\nof the nature and purposes of the right, and in\nparticular focusing on the fact that the only possible\nremedy for the speedy trial violation is a remedy that\nuniquely advantages defendants and poses substantial\nburdens on society, we think ultimately a defendant\ncan't escape responsibility for continuances that he has\nrequested through or without the assistance of counsel,\nsimply by blaming the prosecution for failing to object"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Well, what if the -- what\nif we change the facts slightly. What if the defendant\nhimself never says anything on the record? The only\nthing we've got on the record are repeated requests by\npaid counsel for continuances, and then after three\nyears the defendant fires paid counsel and says: I've\nbeen sitting in jail for three years awaiting trial, and\nI shouldn't have to wait that long. The State has\nfailed in its non-delegable duty. Would your answer\nstill be the same?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. KRUGER",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. KRUGER: It would still be the same. We\nthink that fundamentally the principal safeguard against\nthat kind of unreasonable attorney-caused delay is the\nattorney's ethical obligation, which creates a duty in\nthe attorney to represent his clients with reasonable\ndiligence and promptness. We think that a rule that\nwould require --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: What does your -- your argument do to the fundamental and I think quite vital principle in Polk County that public defenders work for their clients; they don't work for the State?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: But the Chief Justice's question, I'm interested in it as well, are there instances in which a State-appointed counsel can delay and have that not attributed to the State? Is it always attributed to the State --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "4b7756ab59de8d030fa5f55d7a49978787d94fe59d9a5b054441f13f20f6ecdb",
      "evidence_id": "ev-4b7756ab59de-0002-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2008-08-88",
